 class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-8" href="/how-capillary-electrophoresis-analysis-works-in-bird-sexing-and-parentage-testing/#Common_Errors_and_How_Labs_Control_Them" >Common Errors and How Labs Control Them</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-9" href="/how-capillary-electrophoresis-analysis-works-in-bird-sexing-and-parentage-testing/#FAQ" >FAQ</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-10" href="/how-capillary-electrophoresis-analysis-works-in-bird-sexing-and-parentage-testing/#Key_References" >Key References</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-11" href="/how-capillary-electrophoresis-analysis-works-in-bird-sexing-and-parentage-testing/#The_Bottom_Line" >The Bottom Line</a></li></ul></nav></div>
<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_%E2%80%9CCapillary_Electrophoresis_Analysis%E2%80%9D_Means"></span>What "Capillary Electrophoresis Analysis" Means<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">"Capillary electrophoresis analysis" is the entire process of <em>reading</em> DNA — separating fragments by size, measuring them against a known standard, and interpreting the resulting pattern. It is distinct from PCR, which <em>copies</em> the DNA.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If PCR is the photocopier that makes thousands of copies of a specific passage, CE analysis is the reader that measures exactly how long each copy is and tells you what it means.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="The_Physics_and_Chemistry_in_Plain_Language"></span>The Physics and Chemistry in Plain Language<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">CE separates DNA with two forces:</p>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Charge</strong> — DNA's phosphate backbone is negatively charged, so it migrates toward the positive electrode under voltage.</li>



<li><strong>Size sieving</strong> — a viscous polymer inside the capillary acts as an obstacle course; small fragments wiggle through faster, large ones lag.</li>
</ol>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The capillary's tiny diameter (thinner than a human hair) dissipates heat efficiently. This permits high field strengths — <strong>faster runs and sharper separation</strong> than a bulky gel slab can achieve. Fluorescent labels on the DNA are excited by a laser as each fragment passes the detector; the emitted light becomes the electropherogram.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Step-by-Step_From_PCR_Product_to_Result"></span>Step-by-Step: From PCR Product to Result<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Label</strong> — during PCR, primers carry fluorescent dyes, tagging every amplified fragment.</li>



<li><strong>Mix with standard</strong> — dilute product, add an internal size standard (known lengths, different color) and denaturing formamide.</li>



<li><strong>Inject</strong> — a high-voltage pulse pulls a nanoliter-scale sample into the capillary.</li>



<li><strong>Separate</strong> — the field drives fragments through the polymer; small fragments arrive first.</li>



<li><strong>Detect</strong> — laser excites the dyes; a camera records fluorescence over time.</li>



<li><strong>Analyze</strong> — software converts migration time to base-pair size via the standard, plots peaks, and calls the result.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Reading_an_Electropherogram_The_Peaks_Explained"></span>Reading an Electropherogram (The Peaks Explained)<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An electropherogram plots <strong>fragment size (base pairs) on the x-axis</strong> against <strong>fluorescence intensity on the y-axis</strong>.</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Peak position</strong> = fragment length (bp)</li>



<li><strong>Peak height/area</strong> = relative quantity</li>



<li><strong>Color</strong> = dye channel (separates sample fragments from the size standard, and multiplexed markers from each other)</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Software — or a trained analyst — reads the peak pattern to reach a biological conclusion.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Application_1_Bird_Sexing_via_the_CHD_Gene"></span>Application 1: Bird Sexing via the CHD Gene<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bird sexing exploits the CHD gene, present on both sex chromosomes but differing slightly in size between them:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Males (ZZ)</strong> carry two Z copies of CHD → PCR yields <strong>one fragment size</strong> → <strong>one peak</strong>.</li>



<li><strong>Females (ZW)</strong> carry a Z copy (CHD-Z) and a W copy (CHD-W) with a small intron difference → PCR yields <strong>two fragment sizes</strong> → <strong>two peaks</strong>.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This mirrors gel sexing logic (one band = male, two bands = female) but with far better resolution — decisive because the CHD-Z/CHD-W gap is often only a few base pairs and varies across species (Griffiths et al., 1998; Fridolfsson & Ellegren, 1999).</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Why resolution matters</strong>: on coarse agarose, a female's two close fragments can co-migrate into a single smeared band — risking a false male call. CE's 1–2 bp resolution separates them cleanly and reproducibly.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Application_2_Parentage_Identity_via_STR_Markers"></span>Application 2: Parentage & Identity via STR Markers<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Parentage and individual identity (a bird's "DNA passport") use <strong>STR (short tandem repeat) markers</strong> — loci where a short motif repeats a variable number of times. Each bird inherits one allele per marker from each parent, producing a unique profile.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">CE is STR analysis's native platform:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>A panel of STR markers is amplified in <strong>multiplex PCR</strong>, each marker in a different dye.</li>



<li>CE separates and sizes the fragments.</li>



<li>Software compares sizes against an <strong>allelic ladder</strong> (reference of known allele lengths).</li>



<li>Output is a profile like "Marker A: 142/148, Marker B: 210/214…" — unique to that bird.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This precise, digital, repeatable sizing is what makes a DNA parentage certificate defensible, using the same methodology as forensic human identity testing (Butler, 2015).</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Why_CE_Analysis_Is_Reliable"></span>Why CE Analysis Is Reliable<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Internal size standard in every sample</strong> — corrects run-to-run migration variation, enabling ±0.5 bp precision.</li>



<li><strong>Closed, automated workflow</strong> — minimal post-PCR human handling reduces contamination and error.</li>



<li><strong>Digital, traceable output</strong> — the electropherogram is a permanent, auditable record.</li>



<li><strong>Quantitative signal</strong> — peak height flags weak or failed samples for retesting.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These features let quality labs back results with ~20% routine retesting while still turning samples around in 1–2 working days.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Common_Errors_and_How_Labs_Control_Them"></span>Common Errors and How Labs Control Them<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Cause</th><th>Control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>False male (missed second peak)</td><td>Poor resolution or degraded W-copy DNA</td><td>High-resolution CE + fresh follicles</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-contamination</td><td>DNA transfer between samples</td><td>Closed CE + separate pre/post-PCR areas</td></tr><tr><td>Off-ladder alleles</td><td>Rare STR variants absent from ladder</td><td>Software flags for manual review</td></tr><tr><td>Stutter peaks</td><td>PCR slippage at STR repeats</td><td>Software subtracts known stutter patterns</td></tr><tr><td>Sample degradation</td><td>Old or shed feathers</td><td>"Freshly plucked, intact follicle" policy</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="FAQ"></span>FAQ<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>What is an electropherogram?</strong><br>The digital graph CE produces: peaks are DNA fragments, peak position = size, peak height = quantity. It is the traceable raw data behind a sexing or parentage result.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>How does CE tell a male from a female bird?</strong><br>CE sizes amplified CHD fragments. Males (ZZ) give one fragment size (one peak); females (ZW) give two sizes (two peaks) due to the small CHD-Z/CHD-W intron difference.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Why is CE better than gel for bird sexing?</strong><br>CE resolves 1–2 bp, cleanly separating the small CHD-Z/CHD-W difference even when the gap is only a few base pairs; it also automates the workflow, cutting contamination and human error.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>How is CE used in parentage testing?</strong><br>CE sizes fluorescent STR markers against an allelic ladder, producing a precise digital profile used to confirm or exclude parentage.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>How accurate is CE-based bird DNA testing?</strong><br>Professional labs achieve >99% accuracy when CE is paired with validated assays and quality samples, supported by internal QC and routine retesting.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>How long does CE analysis take?</strong><br>The separation run is typically 30–60 minutes; multi-capillary systems analyze many samples in parallel automatically.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Key_References"></span>Key References<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Griffiths, R., Double, M. C., Orr, K., & Dawson, R. J. G. (1998). <em>A DNA test to sex most birds.</em> Molecular Ecology, 7(8), 1071–1075.</li>



<li>Fridolfsson, A.-K., & Ellegren, H. (1999). <em>A simple and universal method for molecular sexing of non-ratite birds.</em> Journal of Avian Biology, 30(1), 116–121.</li>



